---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { check, coverage, summary } from '../lib/data';
import { longDate } from '../lib/format';
---
<Base title="How we know" description="How the Galamsey Tracker finds cleared land from satellite data, what it can and cannot tell you, and what we tried that did not work.">
  <article class="wrap prose">
    <h1>How we know</h1>

    <p>Five satellites photograph Ghana's southern gold belt. Two are American: Landsat 8 and Landsat 9, run by NASA and the US Geological Survey. Three are European: Sentinel-2A, 2B and 2C, run by the European Space Agency for the European Union's Copernicus programme.</p>
    <p>NASA puts their pictures together into one series. A NASA project called OPERA then compares each new picture of a spot with what that spot normally looks like. When most of the plants there disappear and stay gone, OPERA marks it as land that lost its plant cover.</p>
    <p>With five satellites passing over, the same spot gets photographed about every three days. In the rainy season, from June to September, clouds often hide the ground for weeks, so there are fewer clear pictures.</p>
    <p>Every Monday we collect those marks for the last 12 months. Where marked spots touch and add up to at least 1 hectare, about 1.4 football pitches, we call that a patch.</p>
    <p>For each patch we do three more things.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>We check it against the Minerals Commission's public register of licences, to see what kind of licence, if any, covers that land.</li>
      <li>We name it after the nearest town or village and its district.</li>
      <li>For the larger patches, we find a clear Sentinel-2 satellite picture from before the clearing and a recent one, so you can see the change yourself.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Satellites see that plants are gone. They cannot see who removed them or why, and farms, roads and building sites clear land too. So we show you the pictures and the licence on the land, and you can judge each place for yourself.</p>

    <h2 id="mining">Is it all mining?</h2>
    <p>Not all of it, but most. In the pictures, mining for gold usually looks like this:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Pits, often in rows or clusters, many of them filled with water</li>
      <li>Ponds of brown, orange or green water</li>
      <li>Bare yellow or orange ground where there was forest or farmland</li>
      <li>Often along a river or stream, which may turn brown downstream</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Other clearing looks different. Farms are usually neat blocks that turn green again within months. Building sites sit at the edge of towns, with straight roads and roofs.</p>
    {check.patches > 0 && <p>We looked at {check.patches} patches by eye, in before and after pictures: some of the largest and a random sample of the rest. {check.mining} of them showed signs of mining.{check.patches > check.mining && ' The others were hidden by cloud, or looked like farms or building sites.'} We repeat this check on a new sample before we add a new area, because at the edges of the gold belt more of the clearing is farming.</p>}

    <h2>How we compare years</h2>
    <p>Each month we add up the land cleared over the 12 months before, measured on the same day each month. That way each total can be compared fairly with the one a year earlier.</p>
    <p>NASA confirms clearing only after it has seen the same spot clear several times, which can take weeks, or months in the rainy season. So we check each monthly total again two and four months later. Until then it is marked as still being confirmed, and it usually goes up.</p>

    <h2 id="covers">What it covers</h2>
    <p>The tracker does not cover all of Ghana yet. We add areas one at a time, each after checking a sample of its patches by eye. So far it covers {coverage.length === 1 ? 'one area' : `${coverage.length} areas`} of the southern gold belt:</p>
    <ul>
      {coverage.map((a) => <li>{a.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + a.slice(1)}</li>)}
    </ul>
    <p>Within those areas we only count clearing in the forest zone, where the gold belts are. When we checked clearing north of the forest, in the Afram Plains, and on the coastal plain near Kasoa and Winneba, almost none of it was mining. It was farms, burnt bush, building sites and lake shore left bare when Lake Volta fell.</p>
    <p>Within the forest zone, the tracker misses:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Clearing smaller than 1 hectare</li>
      <li>Places hidden by cloud in every picture for a whole year</li>
      <li>Mining on rivers, such as dredging from boats, because it removes no plants</li>
      <li>Clearing that started more than 12 months ago, though new clearing at its edges is counted</li>
    </ul>
    {summary && <p>Latest data: {longDate(summary.data_date)}.</p>}

    <h2>What we tried that did not work</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Radar to see through clouds.</strong> We tested a published method that spots mining ponds in Sentinel-1 radar images. Checked against a published map of mining in Ghana, only about 6% of the pixels it flagged were mining. We do not use it.</li>
      <li><strong>River colour.</strong> We measured how muddy rivers look from space. The change from one picture to the next was bigger than the change from one year to the next, so it cannot show a trend. Rivers are brown in almost every clear picture.</li>
      <li><strong>NASA's yearly summary.</strong> NASA also publishes a yearly version of the same data. Its first year includes a backlog and its latest year is published before late clearing is confirmed, which made it look as if clearing had halved. We use the monthly method above instead.</li>
      <li><strong>New water.</strong> We looked for new ponds in NASA's surface water data. It missed them, probably because most mining ponds are smaller than its 30 metre pixels.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The technical details</h2>
    <dl class="facts">
      <dt>Satellites</dt><dd>Landsat 8 and 9 (NASA and USGS) and Sentinel-2A, 2B and 2C (ESA, Copernicus), combined by NASA as Harmonized Landsat and Sentinel-2 (HLS). The before and after pictures are Sentinel-2 only.</dd>
      <dt>Clearing data</dt><dd>NASA OPERA DIST-ALERT-HLS version 1, 30 metre pixels, Harmonized Landsat and Sentinel-2. We count pixels with status 6 or 8 (confirmed loss of at least 50% of plant cover, ongoing or finished) first detected in the last 365 days.</dd>
      <dt>Patches</dt><dd>Pixels joined with their eight neighbours. At least 1 hectare. A patch seen from two overlapping tiles is kept once.</dd>
      <dt>Forest zone</dt><dd>A patch counts only if it lies in the Eastern Guinean forests ecoregion of RESOLVE Ecoregions 2017. This leaves out the forest-savanna mosaic and coastal savanna, about 4% of the cleared land.</dd>
      <dt>Licences</dt><dd>Active licences from the Minerals Commission cadastre. A patch takes the licence class that covers the largest share of it, if that share is at least 25%. Restricted leases for granite, sand or salt count as no licence to mine gold.</dd>
      <dt>Names</dt><dd>Nearest OpenStreetMap town within 15 km or village within 8 km. Districts from geoBoundaries.</dd>
      <dt>Pictures</dt><dd>Copernicus Sentinel-2 Level 2A from Earth Search. The clearest picture in the 15 months before the patch was first seen, and the clearest since its last change. Pictures with more than 10% cloud or shadow are not used.</dd>
      <dt>Place references</dt><dd>Each patch keeps a permanent reference made from the month it was first seen and its location. When two patches grow into one, the older reference is kept and the other redirects to it.</dd>
    </dl>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose { max-width: calc(68ch + 2 * var(--gutter)); padding-bottom: 24px; }
  h1 { font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 3.4vw, 2.5rem); line-height: 1.1; letter-spacing: -0.02em; margin: 32px 0 24px; }
  h2 { font-size: 1.375rem; line-height: 1.2; margin: 40px 0 10px; }
  p, li { margin: 0 0 14px; }
  ul { padding-left: 1.2em; }
  .facts { gap: 12px 20px; }
  @media (max-width: 560px) { .facts { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 2px; } .facts dd { margin-bottom: 12px; } }
</style>
